### Bug description

`RelationalModelExtensions.GetRelationalModel(IModel)` can throw `InvalidOperationException: The model must be finalized and its runtime dependencies must be initialized before 'GetRelationalModel' can be used` when two threads make the **first** call for the same model at the same time.

Since EF 9 (#33650, "Make RelationalModel lazy"), the relational model is built on first use. The method reads two runtime annotations without synchronization between the reads:

```csharp
// src/EFCore.Relational/Extensions/RelationalModelExtensions.cs (v10.0.12, unchanged on main)
public static IRelationalModel GetRelationalModel(this IModel model)
{
    var relationalModel = (IRelationalModel?)model.FindRuntimeAnnotationValue(RelationalAnnotationNames.RelationalModel);
    if (relationalModel == null)
    {
        var relationalModelFactory = (Func<IRelationalModel>?)model.FindRuntimeAnnotationValue(
                RelationalAnnotationNames.RelationalModelFactory)
            ?? throw new InvalidOperationException(CoreStrings.ModelNotFinalized(nameof(GetRelationalModel)));
        lock (relationalModelFactory)
        {
            relationalModel = model.GetOrAddRuntimeAnnotationValue(
                RelationalAnnotationNames.RelationalModel, f => f!(), relationalModelFactory);
            model.RemoveRuntimeAnnotation(RelationalAnnotationNames.RelationalModelFactory);
        }
    }

    return relationalModel;
}
```

The failing interleaving:

1. Thread B reads `RelationalModel` and gets `null`.
2. Thread A completes the whole first touch: it stores `RelationalModel` and removes `RelationalModelFactory`.
3. Thread B reads `RelationalModelFactory`, gets `null`, and throws `ModelNotFinalized`, although the model is fully built.

The `lock` only protects threads that already hold the factory; the throw happens before it. After the first touch completes, every call takes the fast path, so this only happens once per model object.

### Where we hit it

Integration tests run many test classes in parallel in one process, and each migrates its own database with `context.Database.MigrateAsync()`, using contexts of the same type (same provider configuration, different connection strings).

- Those contexts share one internal service provider, so they also share one cached design-time model.
- `Migrator.ValidateMigrations` calls `HasPendingModelChanges()`, which calls `_designTimeModel.Model.GetRelationalModel()`. That is the first touch of the shared design-time model.
- Several classes reaching it at the same moment occasionally fail. In our runs it failed once in a few thousand cases, under CPU oversubscription.

```text
System.InvalidOperationException : The model must be finalized and its runtime dependencies must be initialized before 'GetRelationalModel' can be used. Ensure that either 'OnModelCreating' has completed or, if using a stand-alone 'ModelBuilder', that 'IModelRuntimeInitializer.Initialize(model.FinalizeModel())' was called.
   at Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.RelationalModelExtensions.GetRelationalModel(IModel model)
   at Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Migrations.Internal.Migrator.HasPendingModelChanges()
   at Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Migrations.Internal.Migrator.ValidateMigrations(Boolean useTransaction, String targetMigration)
   at Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Migrations.Internal.Migrator.MigrateAsync(String targetMigration, CancellationToken cancellationToken)
   at Npgsql.EntityFrameworkCore.PostgreSQL.Migrations.Internal.NpgsqlMigrator.MigrateAsync(String targetMigration, CancellationToken cancellationToken)
   at (application code calling context.Database.MigrateAsync())
```

The runtime model gets the same lazy `RelationalModelFactory` (`RelationalModelRuntimeInitializer`), so as far as we can tell two concurrent first queries or `SaveChanges` calls on a context type right after application start can hit the same race. We have not observed that path.

### Your code

A deterministic reproduction. A `RuntimeModel` subclass holds thread B between its two reads while thread A completes the first touch. It uses internal API (EF1001) only to force the interleaving; the race itself needs no internal API.

```xml
<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <NoWarn>EF1001</NoWarn>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Relational" Version="10.0.12" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
```

```csharp
using Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore;
using Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Infrastructure;
using Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Metadata;
using Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Metadata.Internal;

var model = new PausingModel();
var relational = new RelationalModel(model);
model.AddRuntimeAnnotation(RelationalAnnotationNames.RelationalModelFactory, (Func<IRelationalModel>)(() => relational));
Exception? failure = null;
var b = new Thread(() =>
{
    try { model.GetRelationalModel(); }
    catch (Exception e) { failure = e; }
});
model.Held = b;
b.Start();
model.Reached.Wait();                 // B has read RelationalModel == null
var a = model.GetRelationalModel();   // A: the first touch completes
model.Release.Set();
b.Join();
Console.WriteLine($"A got the relational model: {ReferenceEquals(a, relational)}");
Console.WriteLine($"B: {failure?.GetType().Name}: {failure?.Message}");

internal sealed class PausingModel : RuntimeModel
{
    public Thread? Held;
    public readonly ManualResetEventSlim Reached = new();
    public readonly ManualResetEventSlim Release = new();

    public override Annotation? FindRuntimeAnnotation(string name)
    {
        var annotation = base.FindRuntimeAnnotation(name);
        if (name == RelationalAnnotationNames.RelationalModel && annotation is null && Thread.CurrentThread == Held)
        {
            Reached.Set();
            Release.Wait();
        }

        return annotation;
    }
}
```

Output:

```text
A got the relational model: True
B: InvalidOperationException: The model must be finalized and its runtime dependencies must be initialized before 'GetRelationalModel' can be used. Ensure that either 'OnModelCreating' has completed or, if using a stand-alone 'ModelBuilder', that 'IModelRuntimeInitializer.Initialize(model.FinalizeModel())' was called.
```

### Suggested fix

Re-read `RelationalModel` when the factory is gone, before throwing. For example:

```csharp
var relationalModel = (IRelationalModel?)model.FindRuntimeAnnotationValue(RelationalAnnotationNames.RelationalModel);
if (relationalModel == null)
{
    var relationalModelFactory = (Func<IRelationalModel>?)model.FindRuntimeAnnotationValue(
        RelationalAnnotationNames.RelationalModelFactory);
    if (relationalModelFactory == null)
    {
        // another thread may have completed the first touch between the two reads
        return (IRelationalModel?)model.FindRuntimeAnnotationValue(RelationalAnnotationNames.RelationalModel)
            ?? throw new InvalidOperationException(CoreStrings.ModelNotFinalized(nameof(GetRelationalModel)));
    }

    lock (relationalModelFactory)
    {
        relationalModel = model.GetOrAddRuntimeAnnotationValue(
            RelationalAnnotationNames.RelationalModel, f => f!(), relationalModelFactory);
        model.RemoveRuntimeAnnotation(RelationalAnnotationNames.RelationalModelFactory);
    }
}

return relationalModel;
```

Our workaround is to make the first `GetRelationalModel()` call for each design-time and runtime model one thread at a time:
- under a process-wide lock before migrating;
- for every registered context once at host start, before the host serves requests.

