### Bug description

| Query | Result |
|---|---|
| `GroupBy(e => e.DepartmentName).Select(g => g.Count())` (server-side `GROUP BY`) | `Engineering`: 4 |
| `GroupBy(e => e.DepartmentName).Select(g => g.Select(e => e.Name).ToList())` (lifted) | `Engineering`: 3, `engineering`: 1 |

Before #38963, the lifted projection was translated as a `GROUP BY` plus a `LEFT JOIN` back to the source on key equality. Both used the database's collation, so it returned a single grouping of 4.

There's no exception: the query silently returns more groupings than the database has groups. Case is only one trigger. Anything that makes CLR equality and database equality differ has the same effect:
- SQL Server's trailing-space rule (`'abc' = 'abc '`)
- accent-insensitive collations
- value converters

SQL Server gives string **key** columns a `CaseInsensitiveValueComparer` (#29955). So grouping by a primary, foreign or alternate key column happens to match a case-insensitive collation for case differences, but not for the other triggers. Plain columns like the one below use ordinal comparison.


### Your code

```csharp
using Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore;

await using var context = new AppContext();
await context.Database.EnsureDeletedAsync();
await context.Database.EnsureCreatedAsync();

context.Employees.AddRange(
    new Employee { Name = "Alice", DepartmentName = "Engineering" },
    new Employee { Name = "Bob", DepartmentName = "Engineering" },
    new Employee { Name = "Cheng", DepartmentName = "Engineering" },
    new Employee { Name = "Fen", DepartmentName = "engineering" },
    new Employee { Name = "Dara", DepartmentName = "Sales" });
await context.SaveChangesAsync();

// Server-side GROUP BY: 2 groups (Engineering: 4, Sales: 1)
var groupCounts = await context.Employees
    .GroupBy(e => e.DepartmentName)
    .Select(g => g.Count())
    .ToListAsync();

// Lifted element projection: 3 groupings (3, 1, 1)
var groupings = await context.Employees
    .GroupBy(e => e.DepartmentName)
    .Select(g => g.Select(e => e.Name).ToList())
    .ToListAsync();

Console.WriteLine($"GROUP BY: {string.Join(", ", groupCounts)}");
Console.WriteLine($"Lifted:   {string.Join(", ", groupings.Select(g => g.Count))}");

public class AppContext : DbContext
{
    public DbSet<Employee> Employees => Set<Employee>();

    protected override void OnConfiguring(DbContextOptionsBuilder optionsBuilder)
        => optionsBuilder
            .UseSqlServer(@"Server=(localdb)\mssqllocaldb;Database=GroupByCollationRepro;Trusted_Connection=True")
            .LogTo(Console.WriteLine, Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.LogLevel.Information);
}

public class Employee
{
    public int Id { get; set; }
    public string Name { get; set; } = null!;
    public string DepartmentName { get; set; } = null!;
}
```

Expected: the lifted query returns one grouping per group returned by `GROUP BY` (here 4 and 1).

Actual: it returns 3, 1 and 1.

