### Bug description

After `dotnet ef dbcontext optimize --precompile-queries --nativeaot`, a query that materializes a property mapped with a value converter *class* throws `InvalidCastException`, under the JIT (`dotnet run`) and in the native AOT binary alike. The same program without the compiled model and the precompiled queries works.

On 10.0.12 this affects:

- an application's converter class, configured as `HasConversion<RatingConverter>()`, `HasConversion(new RatingConverter())` or `Properties<Rating>().HaveConversion<RatingConverter>()` in `ConfigureConventions`;
- a built-in converter class: `HasConversion<string>()` on an enum, i.e. `EnumToStringConverter<TEnum>`;
- keys of such a type, e.g. strongly typed IDs: a tracking query fails in the identity-map lookup (`queryContext.TryGetEntry(...)`), a no-tracking query on the key read.

Each of these was checked by changing the corresponding line of the repro below (for the key, an `Id` of type `PostId` mapped with `HasConversion<PostIdConverter>().ValueGeneratedNever()`). Without `--nativeaot`, `HasConversion<RatingConverter>()` works, but `HasConversion(new RatingConverter())` still fails: the compiled model then rebuilds the instance as `SetValueConverter(new ValueConverter<Rating, int>(...))`.

The two halves of the generated code disagree:

- The compiled model rebuilds the type mapping's converter as the base class (`Compiled/PostEntityType.cs`):

  ```csharp
  converter: new ValueConverter<Rating, int>(
      int (Rating r) => r.Value,
      Rating (int value) => new Rating(value)),
  ```

  `CSharpRuntimeAnnotationCodeGenerator.Create(ValueConverter, ...)` writes `new ValueConverter<...>` on 10.0 ([line 388 at v10.0.12](https://github.com/dotnet/efcore/blob/001bfac21c1910cdf1110ca256c9ea0a06f374ca/src/EFCore/Design/Internal/CSharpRuntimeAnnotationCodeGenerator.cs#L388)). On `main` it renders `ConstructorExpressionWithoutMappingHints` ([line 391](https://github.com/dotnet/efcore/blob/7d3f6323fee53e1f34f67639de2eff9d9ab352c2/src/EFCore/Design/Internal/CSharpRuntimeAnnotationCodeGenerator.cs#L391)), which `ValueConverter<TModel, TProvider>` builds from its own constructor, so the subclass is lost either way.

- The precompiled shaper casts the type mapping's converter to the converter's design-time class (`Compiled/Program.EFInterceptors.BlogContext.cs`, line 153):

  ```csharp
  UnsafeAccessor_Post_Rating_Set(instance) = ((RatingConverter)(((IReadOnlyProperty)ratingProperty).GetTypeMapping().Converter)).ConvertFromProviderTyped(dataReader.GetInt32(1));
  ```

  The cast comes from `Convert(converterExpression, converter.GetType())` in `RelationalShapedQueryCompilingExpressionVisitor.ShaperProcessingExpressionVisitor.cs`: lines [3017](https://github.com/dotnet/efcore/blob/001bfac21c1910cdf1110ca256c9ea0a06f374ca/src/EFCore.Relational/Query/RelationalShapedQueryCompilingExpressionVisitor.ShaperProcessingExpressionVisitor.cs#L3017) and [3078](https://github.com/dotnet/efcore/blob/001bfac21c1910cdf1110ca256c9ea0a06f374ca/src/EFCore.Relational/Query/RelationalShapedQueryCompilingExpressionVisitor.ShaperProcessingExpressionVisitor.cs#L3078) at v10.0.12, and [3236](https://github.com/dotnet/efcore/blob/7d3f6323fee53e1f34f67639de2eff9d9ab352c2/src/EFCore.Relational/Query/RelationalShapedQueryCompilingExpressionVisitor.ShaperProcessingExpressionVisitor.cs#L3236), [3299](https://github.com/dotnet/efcore/blob/7d3f6323fee53e1f34f67639de2eff9d9ab352c2/src/EFCore.Relational/Query/RelationalShapedQueryCompilingExpressionVisitor.ShaperProcessingExpressionVisitor.cs#L3299) and [3435](https://github.com/dotnet/efcore/blob/7d3f6323fee53e1f34f67639de2eff9d9ab352c2/src/EFCore.Relational/Query/RelationalShapedQueryCompilingExpressionVisitor.ShaperProcessingExpressionVisitor.cs#L3435) on `main` at 7d3f632. The repro reaches the first one; the others are taken only by converters that set `ConvertsNulls`.

A converter written as lambdas works, because its design-time type is already `ValueConverter<Rating, int>`, so the cast matches. Either side could be changed: cast to the `ValueConverter<TModel, TProvider>` the converter implements (`typedConverterType`, computed just above each site, is enough to call `ConvertFromProviderTyped`), or have the compiled model keep the converter's own class. `AdHocPrecompiledQueryRelationalTestBase.Projecting_entity_with_property_requiring_converter_with_closure_works` uses converter subclasses, but with a model built at run time, which is why it passes.

On 11.0.0-rc.1, `HasConversion<string>()` on an enum works: since #38440 the compiled model refers to a built-in converter's static `Instance` singleton rather than rebuilding it. Any other converter instance still fails: an application's converter class, or a built-in one passed as a new instance, e.g. `HasConversion(new EnumToStringConverter<Status>())`.

Workaround: configure the conversion with lambdas, e.g. `HasConversion(r => r.Value, v => new Rating(v))`, or `HasConversion(v => v.ToString(), v => Enum.Parse<Status>(v))` for an enum. That covers materialization only: a converted value used as a query parameter still fails, see #33541.

