**Environment:** .NET 10.0.11 with EF Core 10.0.13, and .NET 11.0.0-rc.2.26504.105 with EF Core 11.0.0-rc.2.26504.105. Providers Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Sqlite (Microsoft.Data.Sqlite, SQLite 3.53.3 / 3.53.4) and Npgsql.EntityFrameworkCore.PostgreSQL 10.0.3 / 11.0.0-rc.2 (Npgsql 10.0.3, PostgreSQL 18.1). macOS 27.0.1, arm64. Versions read from the loaded assemblies and the servers.

`ComparisonTranslator` translates a call to any `int`-returning method named `Compare` with two arguments of the same type into `CASE WHEN a = b THEN 0 WHEN a > b THEN 1 WHEN a < b THEN -1 END`, the natural order of the column type. It doesn't look at which type declares the method. So `IComparer<T>.Compare` on any comparer instance is translated as if the comparer weren't there. The query runs, raises no error or warning, and returns different rows than the same LINQ over the loaded entities:

| LINQ | SQL (both providers, both versions) | Database | LINQ to Objects |
|---|---|---|---|
| `StringComparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase.Compare(i.Name, "abc") == 0` | `WHERE "i"."Name" = 'abc'` | `[1]` | `[1, 2]` |
| `reversed.Compare(i.Id, 2) > 0`, with `reversed = Comparer<int>.Create((x, y) => y.CompareTo(x))` | `WHERE "i"."Id" > 2` | `[3, 4]` | `[1]` |
| `OrderBy(i => reversed.Compare(i.Id, 2)).ThenBy(i => i.Id)` | `ORDER BY CASE WHEN "i"."Id" = 2 THEN 0 WHEN "i"."Id" > 2 THEN 1 WHEN "i"."Id" < 2 THEN -1 END, "i"."Id"` | `[1, 2, 3, 4]` | `[3, 4, 2, 1]` |

The rows are `1 "abc"`, `2 "ABC"`, `3 "abd"` and `4 "b"`.

We hit this through a library comparer that orders ranges by their lower bound only (CaffeinatedCoder/CodoMetis.ValueRanges#385). Inside a query, Npgsql's provider compared the whole range instead.

