### Suggested fix

Make the comparison total, so an unparseable value is *ordered* rather than rejected. SQLite requires a
collation to be a consistent total order — an inconsistent one gives wrong index lookups — so the
fallback needs to be deterministic and transitive. Ordering non-numeric values after all numeric ones,
and comparing them ordinally among themselves, satisfies that:

```csharp
sqliteConnection.CreateCollation(
    "EF_DECIMAL",
    static (x, y) =>
    {
        var xIsNumber = decimal.TryParse(x, NumberStyles.Number, CultureInfo.InvariantCulture, out var xValue);
        var yIsNumber = decimal.TryParse(y, NumberStyles.Number, CultureInfo.InvariantCulture, out var yValue);

        return xIsNumber && yIsNumber ? decimal.Compare(xValue, yValue)
            : xIsNumber ? -1
            : yIsNumber ? 1
            : string.CompareOrdinal(x, y);
    });
```

Values outside `decimal`'s range (`"1e40"`, a 40-digit integer) also fail to parse today and take the
same path, so this covers them too.

Whatever the chosen ordering semantics, the important part is that nothing thrown escapes the callback —
any managed exception in a collation is an unrecoverable process exit, not a query error the caller can
handle or log.

